  "summary": "Mario Party 10 is NDcube's Wii U party game, first released in Japan on 12 March 2015.",
  "body": [
    "Mario Party 10 is NDcube's Wii U party game, first released in Japan on 12 March 2015. The main board mode continues Mario Party 9's shared vehicle and Mini Stars, while Bowser Party uses the GamePad to create a different contest. Four players cooperate against a fifth person controlling Bowser. The extra screen makes an opposing role possible within the same room, turning the familiar villain into another participant whose attention and controls differ from those of the group trying to escape.",
    "In Bowser Party, the team travels together while Bowser pursues them and triggers special minigames. The GamePad provides the large character's viewpoint and input, while the other players react with their own controllers. The asymmetry gives a short challenge two sides: Bowser tries to exploit the situation, and the group must understand and survive it together. Losing health affects their continued journey, so cooperation extends beyond a single encounter. The player holding the separate screen becomes a continuing source of pressure along the board.",
    "Amiibo Party supplies another board format linked to figures, while the standard Mario Party mode keeps four competitors gathering Mini Stars across five maps. More than seventy minigames connect the different arrangements. Rei Kondoh and Toshiki Aida composed the music. The game explores several Wii U ideas at once, with Bowser Party making the clearest use of its unusual hardware: one person can remain physically beside the others while playing a visibly different part, using a private screen to challenge the shared television view."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Bowser gets the other screen",
    "Four players flee one pursuer",
    "Three ways to use the party board"
  ],
